3.4.m:
matallīskhalita, bhramara, pārśvasvastika, / madādvilasita, vikṣipta, gatimaṇḍala;
vaiśākharecita, parivṛttakarecita, / udvartaka, paricchinna, recaka, then balāhaka;
sambhrānta, vāṅgika, krīḍa, alātaka, / vidyudbhrānta, parāvṛtta, together with pārśvaccheda;
ākṣipta, recita, saumya, then karita, / sūcīviddha, apaviddha, vilāpa, and avanikuṭṭita.
These thirty-six are declared as the aṅgahāras, O best of the Yadus. / I shall explain their basic units in due order, / and how the movement of hands and feet is to be applied by the practitioners.
The basic unit is the production of all the aṅgahāras; / I shall explain them by their names and by their movements.
A karaṇa of dance is combined with [specific movements of] hands and feet; / two dance-karaṇas constitute a mātṛkā, O king.

Lalita, daṇḍapakṣa, bhujaṅgatrastarecita, / nūpura, recita, bhramara, and catura;
bhujaṅgāñcita is another, chinna, vṛścikarecita, / latāvṛścika is another, chinna, vṛścikarecita;
vṛścika, vyaṃsita, and sūcīviddhaka, / lalāṭatilaka, kuṇḍala, cakramaṇḍala;
tarormaṇḍala, ākṣipta, latālasita, argala, / vikṣipta, bhramita, vilāsa, and vānarapluta;
parivṛtta, nivṛtta, pārśvākrānta, nikuñcita, / atikrānta, avakrānta, dolāpādavivarjita;
nāgaprakrīḍita, vipluta, garuḍapluta, / gaṇḍasūcī, parikṣipta, and pārśvajānuka;
gṛhāvalīnaka, līna, viṣṇukrānta, ajapluta, / ākrānta, maṇḍita, mayūralasita, and nasa;
sarpita, colapāda, and hariṇapluta, / preṅkhālita, nitamba, skhalita, and parihastaka;

3.5.m:
In holding a branch, gathering clusters of blossoms, sitting with rolling eyes and lamenting, / and in natural conversation, this posture should be assumed, O foremost of the Yādava lineage.
3.24 / Mārkaṇḍeya said: / Hear from me as I first explain the movement of the head. / When, from its natural straight [position], it is tossed upward once suddenly,
it should be used in gesturing, instructing, questioning, and in natural conversation. / When it is moved repeatedly, it is regarded as kampita.
It is seen in anger, threatening, argumentation, and solemn declarations. / Moving the head slowly to one side is regarded as dhuta;
it is used in despondency, wonder, and prohibition. / Shaking [the head] from side to side is named vidhuta;
it occurs in one afflicted with cold or suffering from fever, in an intoxicated person, and in one who is hurried. / In commencing a circular movement, it is reproached.
It is desired in accomplishment, wonder, joy, and playfulness. / When [the head] is raised upward once, it is regarded as udvāhita;

Thus the action of the head has been described to you, O king of men. / Añcita, recita, mukta, vivṛta, and likewise catura,
prasārita, and likewise stabdha, should be indicated in the movements of the neck. / Añcita is slightly to one shoulder, but from rolling it is recita;
recita is from turning around, mukta [is released], and vivṛta is moved; / catura is declared sweet, and prasārita is extended;
and stabdha is declared rigid, and it follows the movement of the head. / Añcita should be in playful grace and the like, recita in glancing around in a circle;
mukta [should be used] in states of fainting and the like, vivṛtta in graceful movements and the like; / catura in a gentle state, and prasārita should be done in gazing;
stabdha in cold or disease, naturally motionless. / Vibuddha, vinivṛtta, nirbhugna, and also bhugna,
and likewise vivṛtta and ṛju should be indicated in its action. / Turned away is named vibuddha; nivṛtta is covered;

facing downward is avābhugna; turned upward is nibhugna; / bhugna is slightly bent; nivṛtta is opened;
and ṛju rests in its natural state: [now] their actions are spoken of. / It should be performed in warding off what is wrong, in phrases such as 'Not so!',
vinivṛtta in indignation, and in actions born of jealousy and anger; / vibhugna is to be known in profound gazing and the like;
bhugna should be employed in shame, and naturally for ascetics; / nivṛtta also should be performed in laughter, sorrow, fear, and the like;
it should be performed in the natural upright position, O delight of the Yadus. / Ābhugna, then nirbhugna, and likewise prakampita,

With the upper eyelid fallen, tearful, and with pupils lost [from sight], it is the pathetic [gaze]; / with the tips of the eyelashes slightly contracted, and the pupils slightly turned up,
even and wide open, that is held to be the marvellous gaze. / Raised and with crooked knitting of the brows, the furious is described;
with dilated centre and even pupils, the heroic is described. / With the pupils cast toward the outer corners, the odious gaze is described;
unblinking through meditation, moving downward, [such] is the peaceful. / With wide-open centre, sweet, loving, and gently smiling is considered [the loving gaze];
with dilated centre, quivering, joyful, looking toward the periphery [is the joyful gaze]. / With crooked pupils directed downward, concealed, submerged in looking [is the deceitful gaze];
with crooked knitting of the brows, and motionless pupils, is the angry [gaze]. / Contracted and with motionless pupils is the bashful [gaze];
greatly desired by those of noble spirit, wide open, is the proud [gaze]. / Even, with the outer corners expanded, is said to be the astonished [gaze];
even and with even pupils, is declared to be the gentle [gaze]. / With contracted eyelids, rough, and with averted pupils, is the frightened [gaze];

one whose two eyelids quiver is called the dejected [gaze]. / The gaze which has contracted eyelids is the squinting [gaze];
with slightly raised eyelids, that is prescribed as the tranquil [gaze]. / With slow pupils and eyelids, it is the distressed and faint [gaze];
that which is drooping and quivering is called the disturbed [gaze]. / With pupils slightly stiffened, it is called the despondent gaze;
and with unsteady pupils, it is celebrated as the apprehensive [gaze]. / Harsh, with playful pupils, and displaying fear, it is the frightened [gaze];
wide open and with unblinking eyes, it is named the sorrowless [gaze]. / With unsteady eyes and wandering pupils, it is the bewildered [gaze];
with slightly curved pupils, it is the contracted gaze. / With stationary pupils and eyelids, looking evenly, is the vacant [gaze] in agitation;
with trembling tips of the eyelashes, bud-like, is the released [gaze]. / With turned-up eyelids and eyelashes, and rigid pupils, is the reflective [gaze];
wide open and with rolling pupils, it is named the intoxicated [gaze]. / With blossoming corners, charming, and smiling, it is regarded as the graceful [gaze];

with drooping eyelashes, tearful at the tip, and forming a bud, it is called the languid [gaze]. / The lovely gaze is in sidelong glances, but the terrible is in slaying;
the comic is to be known in laughter, and the pathetic in the pathetic sentiment. / In the marvellous, the marvellous [gaze] should be used, and the furious is prescribed in the furious [sentiment];
the heroic should be employed in the heroic [sentiment], and the odious in disgust. / The peaceful is prescribed in the peaceful [sentiment], and the loving in an affectionate glance;
the joyful should be used in delight, the oblique should be used in malice. / The angry is prescribed in rage, and the frightened should likewise be in fear;
the bashful is called for in shame, and the proud in states of energy. / The astonished gaze is in wonder, and the gentle in a gentle glance;
the dejected should be used in separation, and the squinting in love's union. / The weary should be used for one who is exhausted, but the distressed in despair;
the disturbed is in misery, and the despondent in dejection. / The apprehensive is in suspicion, and the frightened is prescribed in terror;

Where the two eyelids are brought together, that is closed; / where the eyelids have their bases dropped, that is called beaten.
In unpleasant smell and touch, contracting should be known by the wise. / For the purpose of wonder and in the heroic, that [elevation] should be performed and perfected;
in the erotic [sentiment], the level should be used, and in a blow, the beaten. / In women's lovers' quarrels, throbbing is desired here;
in cold, wind, heat, and rain, the closed should indeed be performed. / In anger, rolling should be used, and the level in closing and opening.
Turning, revolving, moving, disappearing, / rolling, splitting, and also astonishment,
turning upward, and natural: these are held to be the actions of the pupils. / Circular motion between the eyelids is known as revolving;
turning is sideways movement; from turning comes moving. / Drawing inward is entering, from going within;

and from moving to the outer corner, it is called elated. / Dropping is the condition of sinking down, and issuing forth from going outward;
turning back is from moving upward, and natural is from one's own nature. / In laughter, turning should be used, and revolving in intoxication;
in the heroic sentiment there should be moving, and in the odious, entering; / rolling is in the erotic, and dropping in the pathetic sentiment;
issuing forth is in the terrible, and turning upward in the heroic. / In the remaining states, one should employ the natural as appropriate.
There is the straight, and the sidelong; after this, the following; / looking, looking upward, and looking downward,
with level pupils and gentle: that is the straight eye-movement. / With pupils gone inside the eyelashes: that is called sidelong here;
employed in delineating forms, it is known as following. / What is seeing suddenly is held to be looking;
looking upward is known as gazing up, and looking on all sides [is surveying]. / Looking to the two sides is viewing sideways, and looking behind is looking downward.

And one should employ the action of the eyebrows following the glances; / for indeed the eye glides along, resting on various emotions and rasas.
The movement of the eyebrows should indeed occur in accordance with the eye: / raising, falling, knitting the brow, and charming;
contracted, curled, and also natural. / Raising the eyebrows is utkṣepa (raising); lowering is patana (falling).
From raising the root of both [eyebrows] upward, bhrukuṭī (knitting the brow) is declared. / Catura (charming) is from a slight elevation of a sweet, elongated eyebrow.
Of one or both there may be udbhagna (broken) or trikuñcita (triply curved); / from the graceful movement of one, it is proclaimed by name as recita (emptied).
Learn from me the function of the eyebrows in their proper place and natural state: / raising [is used] in amorous sport, falling from compassion;
and the knitting of the brow is to be known in anger, and catura in the erotic [sentiment]. / [The cheek] is to be known as kṣāma (wasted) and thin, phulla (blossoming) is expanded;

sampūrṇa (full) is called filled out, kampita (trembling) is shaken; / śṛṅgabhinna (pointed/furrowed) is contracted, and saromāñca (horripilating) is of such kind;
the natural state is called prākṛta (ordinary) and manda (calm) by the wise. / Wasted should be employed in grief, phulla in joy;
full should be made in satisfaction, and trembling in rage; / contracted is prescribed in laughter, and horripilating in touch;
and the ordinary, resting in its natural state, one should employ according to the connection of meaning. / Bent, sluggish, drawn, panting, wrinkled, turned down—
and in its natural state should the nose be executed in performance by the wise. / Turned down is to be known as natā, and sluggish has drooping nostrils;
drawn is contracted and raised upward, panting is filled with breath; / from contracting it is kūṇitā (wrinkled), and one's nose resting in its normal condition is svabhāvasthā (natural).
The turned-down [nose] should be used in what is pleasing, sluggish is prescribed in grief; / drawn in strong fragrance, and panting in beloved [encounters];
